        "updatedAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z"
    },
    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2026-05-09T09:45:10Z",
    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Yes, this is possible via a custom `MiniBufremove.wipeout()` wrapper. Like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal my_wipeout = function()\r\n  if #vim.api.nvim_list_tabpages() > 1 and #vim.api.nvim_tabpage_list_wins(0) then vim.cmd('quit') end\r\n  MiniBufremove.wipeout()\r\nend\r\nvim.keymap.set('n', '<Leader>bw', my_wipeout)\r\n```\r\n\r\nCouple of notes:\r\n- This will not be part of 'mini.bufremove' as its whole point is to have a way to wipeout/delete a buffer while preserving window layout. Closing a window/tabpage is a layout change.\r\n- The \"Buffer that is being closed is the only buffer in the current tab\" is a bit inaccurate. From `:h windows-intro`:\r\n\r\n    > A buffer is the in-memory text of a file.\r\n    > A window is a viewport on a buffer.\r\n    > A tab page is a collection of windows.\r\n\r\n    So it will be more accurate to say \"The current window that displays the current buffer is the only one in the current tabpage\". But this also shows a small problem with the initial question: there can be more windows that show the buffer-to-be-wiped-out. Even in the current tabpage. So that should be taken into account either as a known limitation or by making a wrapper more complex.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Yes, this is possible via a custom <code class=\"notranslate\">MiniBufremove.wipeout()</code> wrapper. Like this:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"local my_wipeout = function()\n  if #vim.api.nvim_list_tabpages() &gt; 1 and #vim.api.nvim_tabpage_list_wins(0) then vim.cmd('quit') end\n  MiniBufremove.wipeout()\nend\nvim.keymap.set('n', '&lt;Leader&gt;bw', my_wipeout)\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-en\">my_wipeout</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>()\n  <span class=\"pl-k\">if</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">#</span><span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">api</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">nvim_list_tabpages</span>() <span class=\"pl-k\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">1</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">and</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">#</span><span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">api</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">nvim_tabpage_list_wins</span>(<span class=\"pl-c1\">0</span>) <span class=\"pl-k\">then</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">cmd</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>quit<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>) <span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n  <span class=\"pl-smi\">MiniBufremove</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">wipeout</span>()\n<span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n<span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">keymap</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">set</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>n<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>&lt;Leader&gt;bw<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">my_wipeout</span>)</pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Couple of notes:</p>\n<ul dir=\"auto\">\n<li>\n<p dir=\"auto\">This will not be part of 'mini.bufremove' as its whole point is to have a way to wipeout/delete a buffer while preserving window layout. Closing a window/tabpage is a layout change.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The \"Buffer that is being closed is the only buffer in the current tab\" is a bit inaccurate. From <code class=\"notranslate\">:h windows-intro</code>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">A buffer is the in-memory text of a file.<br>\nA window is a viewport on a buffer.<br>\nA tab page is a collection of windows.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">So it will be more accurate to say \"The current window that displays the current buffer is the only one in the current tabpage\". But this also shows a small problem with the initial question: there can be more windows that show the buffer-to-be-wiped-out. Even in the current tabpage. So that should be taken into account either as a known limitation or by making a wrapper more complex.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>",
            "bodyText": "Yes, this is possible via a custom MiniBufremove.wipeout() wrapper. Like this:\nlocal my_wipeout = function()\n  if #vim.api.nvim_list_tabpages() > 1 and #vim.api.nvim_tabpage_list_wins(0) then vim.cmd('quit') end\n  MiniBufremove.wipeout()\nend\nvim.keymap.set('n', '<Leader>bw', my_wipeout)\nCouple of notes:\n\n\nThis will not be part of 'mini.bufremove' as its whole point is to have a way to wipeout/delete a buffer while preserving window layout. Closing a window/tabpage is a layout change.\n\n\nThe \"Buffer that is being closed is the only buffer in the current tab\" is a bit inaccurate. From :h windows-intro:\n\nA buffer is the in-memory text of a file.\nA window is a viewport on a buffer.\nA tab page is a collection of windows.\n\nSo it will be more accurate to say \"The current window that displays the current buffer is the only one in the current tabpage\". But this also shows a small problem with the initial question: there can be more windows that show the buffer-to-be-wiped-out. Even in the current tabpage. So that should be taken into account either as a known limitation or by making a wrapper more complex.",
